WEBVTT

00:00.300 --> 00:03.580
Planning a dive: twenty metres, thirty-five minutes.

00:03.580 --> 00:06.060
Will your tank cover it, with a reserve left?

00:06.060 --> 00:07.950
Work that out before you get in.

00:09.050 --> 00:16.750
The gas a dive needs is three things multiplied: your surface consumption rate, SCR, the litres you breathe in a minute at the surface;

00:16.750 --> 00:18.690
the pressure at your average depth;

00:18.770 --> 00:19.840
and the time.

00:22.140 --> 00:30.060
Our example: twenty metres average depth, thirty-five minutes, an eleven point one litre tank, a rate of fifteen litres a minute.

00:31.780 --> 00:35.280
Step one, the pressure: twenty over ten, plus one.

00:35.280 --> 00:36.430
Three atmospheres.

00:37.680 --> 00:40.360
Step two: fifteen litres a minute, times three.

00:40.700 --> 00:42.850
Forty-five litres a minute at twenty metres.

00:45.150 --> 00:48.070
Step three: times thirty-five minutes.

00:48.090 --> 00:50.400
Fifteen hundred and seventy-five litres.

00:51.850 --> 00:55.250
Step four, into bar: divide by the tank's litres.

00:55.350 --> 00:56.860
A hundred and forty-two bar.

00:59.160 --> 01:03.180
Check it against the dive as it went: a hundred and forty bar used.

01:03.180 --> 01:05.660
The plan said a hundred and forty-two bar.

01:05.660 --> 01:06.290
It works.

01:07.740 --> 01:14.180
Then the reserve, minimum gas: what two divers need to get up from twenty metres, the deepest point of this dive: sixty bar.

01:14.200 --> 01:17.200
Together, two hundred and two bar: more than the tank holds.

01:19.400 --> 01:22.720
The rule: the dive plus the reserve must fit the fill.

01:22.720 --> 01:25.410
Here: fewer minutes, or a bigger tank.

01:27.710 --> 01:32.270
Turn Pressure does this for your dive: the gas, and the minimum gas.

01:32.450 --> 01:33.770
Free on the App Store.

01:33.770 --> 01:35.400
diver dot today.
